WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.560
Margot Robbie is known for taking on challenging&nbsp;
roles that portray fascinating characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.220
so it should be no surprise that&nbsp;
she had a specific condition for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.059
playing the most recognizable doll in the world.

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:15.960
It might be hard to imagine anybody besides Margot&nbsp;
Robbie as Barbie even though there were originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.120
other actors attached to the long-awaited "Barbie"&nbsp;
movie. Still, according to a new article in Time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.367
Robbie wouldn't have taken the movie&nbsp;
if she was the only one playing Barbie.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:26.700
"Hi, Barbie!"&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.080
"Hi Barbie."
"Hi Barbie."&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.280
"Hi Barbie."
"Hi Barbie!"&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.300
"Hi Barbie!"
"Hi Barbie."

00:00:32.357 --> 00:00:36.240
As the piece explains, Robbie's Barbie&nbsp;
definitely is the traditional version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.040
of the character people remember: long&nbsp;
blonde hair, long tan legs, pink outfits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.020
the works. But after Mattel introduced a much&nbsp;
wider variety of Barbies into its roster, it made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.820
it possible for the movie to depict the world's&nbsp;
most famous doll in many new ways. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.380
Robbie revealed that, without other Barbies, she&nbsp;
wouldn't have been interested in the role, saying,

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.760
"If [Mattel] hadn't made that change to have a&nbsp;
multiplicity of Barbies, I don't think I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.900
have wanted to attempt to make a Barbie film.&nbsp;
I don't think you should say, 'This is the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.866
version of what Barbie is, and that's what women&nbsp;
should aspire to be and look like and act like.'"

00:01:08.866 --> 00:01:12.840
Issa Rae agreed with Robbie, saying that&nbsp;
the sheer diversity of the cast is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.617
helped sell her on the "Barbie" movie as&nbsp;
a whole. Speaking with Time, she said,

00:01:16.617 --> 00:01:20.580
"My worry was that it was going to feel&nbsp;
too white feminist-y, but I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.620
it's self-aware. Barbie Land is perfect, right?&nbsp;
It represents perfection. So if perfection is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.040
just a bunch of white Barbies, I don't know&nbsp;
that anybody can get on board with that."

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.160
The COO and president of Mattel, Richard Dickson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.468
made the argument that for people who only have&nbsp;
one version of Barbie already in their head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:40.980
Robbie's portrayal as the proto-Barbie is&nbsp;
simply a gateway to the others, saying,

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.340
"Of course she looks like Barbie.&nbsp;
But they're all Barbie. It's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.240
perfect cast to express what Barbie&nbsp;
is today. And Margot is the bridge."

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.640
Hari Nef, who plays Doctor Barbie in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.960
came into the project with her lived experience&nbsp;
as a trans woman, and she also felt comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.468
with and excited by the project thanks to&nbsp;
writer-director Greta Gerwig. She said,

00:01:59.468 --> 00:02:02.880
"I knew this was not going to shy away&nbsp;
from the parts of Barbie that are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.440
interesting but potentially a little bit more&nbsp;
fraught. The contemporary history of feminism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.940
and body positivity—there are questions&nbsp;
of how Barbie can fit into all of that."

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.320
As for what the "Barbie" movie is about, a lot&nbsp;
of the plot details have been kept under wraps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.480
but fans of writer-director Greta Gerwig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.920
and her co-writer and real-life partner Noah&nbsp;
Baumbach, know that this movie probably has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.420
plenty of exciting tricks up its bright&nbsp;
pink sleeve. What we do know is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.800
when she starts thinking about death too much&nbsp;
and her arched feet go flat, Robbie's Barbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:37.456
and her Ken head to the real world to figure out&nbsp;
how to solve this admittedly bizarre problem.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.420
"No comment. No seriously, no comment."

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.160
In the Time piece, the cast remained fairly&nbsp;
mum about specific plot details, but Mattel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.780
executives had some comments that turned out to be&nbsp;
pretty interesting. Beyond admitting that they'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.460
always hoped Robbie would play Barbie, executive&nbsp;
producer of Mattel Films Robbie Brenner said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.840
"Barbie" is "not a feminist movie." Robbie&nbsp;
had an interesting response to that, saying,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.460
"It's not that it is or it isn't. It's a&nbsp;
movie. It's a movie that's got so much in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.840
it. [The point is that] we're in on the&nbsp;
joke. This isn't a Barbie puff piece."

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.300
With all the anticipation&nbsp;
building for the "Barbie" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.140
are the powers that be eying a sequel?&nbsp;
It probably won't shock you to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:21.360
that the Mattel executives aren't against the&nbsp;
idea. Mattel CEO Ynon Kreiz specifically says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.680
that there's a possibility of "more Barbie&nbsp;
movies," but Robbie, once again, demurred:

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.000
"It could go a million different directions&nbsp;
from this point. But I think you fall into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.218
bit of a trap if you try and set up a first&nbsp;
movie whilst also planning for sequels."

